/-
Pure γγ words on four strands pass reading 3 near q = 1, with an explicit uniform bound.

Theorem `pure_gg_reading3`: let `w` be a word in the four γγ vertices
`V₀ = σ₁σ₂⁻¹`, `B₀ = σ₁⁻¹σ₂`, `V₁ = σ₂σ₃⁻¹`, `B₁ = σ₂⁻¹σ₃` of length `m ≥ 1` whose underlying
permutation in `S₄` is the identity (a pure braid). Then for every real `q` with
`1 ≤ q ≤ 1 + (1/12)/m`, all eight level characters of `w` (levels 1–4, convention-D weights
`y_λ = [λ₁−λ₂+1]_q / [2]_q^{|λ|}`) are nonnegative.

Method. The generators act on the irreducibles `(4)`, `(3,1)`, `(2,2)` of `H₄(q)` by
Hoefsmit's seminormal matrices (Gelfand–Tsetlin basis). On `(4)` each vertex is `q·q⁻¹ = 1`.
At `q = 1` the matrices factor through `S₄`; the 24 images are tabulated (`F`, `T31`, `T22`),
so a pure word has unperturbed product `1`. A product perturbation estimate in the
`ℓ∞` operator norm (`QOU.MathlibExt.ListProdPerturbation`) bounds
`‖W − 1‖ ≤ M((1 + Mδ)^{2m} − 1) ≤ 1` with `M = 11/6` (sup of the table norms) and
`δ = (11/10)(q − 1)` (per-letter entry bounds, certified by polynomial inequalities on
`q ∈ [1, 11/10]`). Hence every diagonal entry is `≥ 0`, and every level character is a
positive combination of diagonal entries.

Scope. The constant `1/12` is what this argument proves. The measured threshold
(`computations/probes/gg_four_strand/results.md`, amendment 3) is much larger:
`m·(q* − 1) ≥ 1.187` on the lengths tested. A bound of the form `1 + c/m` cannot by itself
reach the proton `q₀ ≈ 1.10998` for long words; this theorem says nothing about that.
No numerical input enters the proof.
